{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AtbvufaNbDe4GV4BkfhTTs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Gap hunt: B03 — Credential lifetime and handling\n2\t\n3\tA reviewer couldn't decide whether this item is met. **Your job is to find out whether any\n4\tcheck below is false in this codebase.** The reviewer's partial answers and the code they\n5\tlooked at are shown. Read the code, and open the repo files the checks point to.\n6\t\n7\tFor each check, decide:\n8\t- **fails**: you found the line that makes it false (a missing control, a bypass, a\n9\t  value never used, an exception that continues, a filter that lets something through).\n10\t  Quote that line;\n11\t- **holds**: you found the code that makes it true in every case;\n12\t- **unclear**: you can't tell from this repo.\n13\t\n14\t**The requirement for B03:** Issue short-lived credentials from a managed identity or secrets service. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where the service supports it, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Then a copied token won't work from another machine. Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. Show that credentials expire and rotate, and explain why you chose their lifetime.\n15\t\n16\tIf a check fails, also copy the words from this requirement that the code breaks into\n17\t`violates`. Then write your answer (format at the end).\n18\t\n19\t\n20\t## B03.1\n21\t\n22\t**Check:** Credentials are fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, not fixed API keys, where short-lived or federated credentials are available.\n23\t\n24\t**How to verify:** 1) Find credential setup (`AssumeRole`, IRSA, Workload Identity, Managed Identity, Vault, `os.environ`, `process.env`). 2) Check each credential is fetched at run time. 3) Answer no if a fixed key (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `API_KEY` in a Secret or env) is used for a service that offers federated or short-lived credentials, or code falls back to a static key when the managed source fails.\n25\t\n26\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n27\t\n28\t**Reviewer's note:** Workload and STS tokens are fetched at run time but the Anthropic API key has no source in this repo, so a fixed key may be in use\n29\t\n30\t(no evidence cited)\n31\t\n32\t## B03.2\n33\t\n34\t**Check:** Credential lifetime and rotation are set in config to bounded values.\n35\t\n36\t**How to verify:** 1) Search for lifetime and rotation settings (`DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`, `max_ttl`, rotation job or policy). 2) Check each credential the agent uses has a bounded value. 3) Answer no if any credential has no expiry, no rotation, or an unbounded or very long value. Answer unknown if lifetimes are set in an identity provider outside this repo.\n37\t\n38\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n39\t\n40\t**Reviewer's note:** Projected token lifetime is bounded but rotation of mounted Slack and mail HMAC secrets is not configured here\n41\t\n42\t(no evidence cited)\n43\t\n44\t## B03.3\n45\t\n46\t**Check:** No long-lived secrets appear in prompts, Dockerfiles or image layers, or checked-in config.\n47\t\n48\t**How to verify:** 1) Search prompt templates, Dockerfiles (`ENV`, `ARG`, `COPY .env`), `.env*`, `*.yaml`, `*.tfvars`, `.mcp.json` `env` blocks for key-like literals (`sk-`, `AKIA`, `token:`, `password:`). 2) Check prompt-building code for env values inserted into messages. 3) Answer no if any real secret is checked in, baked into an image, or placed in a prompt; `.gitignore` listing a file does not count if it is committed.\n49\t\n50\t**Reviewer's evidence:** Dockerfile:12 — \"ENV PYTHONPATH=/opt/site:/app \\\"\n51\t\n52\t**Reviewer's note:** Image env and config hold no secret values\n53\t\n54\t`Dockerfile` lines 1–22 (cited lines marked >):\n55\t```\n56\t   1  # syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.10\n57\t   2  FROM python:3.11.10-slim-bookworm@sha256:2a6386ad2db20e7f55073f69a98d6da2cf9f168e05e7487d2670c22e4ad2a1bd AS build\n58\t   3  WORKDIR /src\n59\t   4  ENV PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1 PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1\n60\t   5  COPY pyproject.toml ./\n61\t   6  COPY app ./app\n62\t   7  RUN pip install --target /opt/site .\n63\t   8  RUN python -m compileall -q app\n64\t   9  \n65\t  10  FROM gcr.io/distroless/python3-debian12:nonroot@sha256:8f3c1a6e2b9d4f7a0c5e8b1d3f6a9c2e5b8d1f4a7c0e3b6d9f2a5c8e1b4d7f0a\n66\t  11  WORKDIR /app\n67\t  12> ENV PYTHONPATH=/opt/site:/app \\\n68\t  13      PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \\\n69\t  14      APP_ROOT=/app\n70\t  15  COPY --from=build /opt/site /opt/site\n71\t  16  COPY --from=build /src/app /app/app\n72\t  17  COPY prompts /app/prompts\n73\t  18  COPY config /app/config\n74\t  19  COPY release/manifest.yaml /app/release/manifest.yaml\n75\t  20  USER 65532:65532\n76\t  21  EXPOSE 8080\n77\t  22  ENTRYPOINT [\"python\", \"-m\", \"uvicorn\", \"app.main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8080\", \"--no-server-header\"]\n78\t```\n79\t\n80\t## B03.4\n81\t\n82\t**Check:** Where the service supports it, tokens are bound to their holder with mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens.\n83\t\n84\t**How to verify:** 1) Find token and client setup for each service (`mTLS`, `client_cert`, `DPoP`, `cert-bound`, SPIFFE). 2) Check whether the service supports holder binding. 3) Answer no if a supporting service uses plain bearer tokens. Answer unknown if support depends on a provider outside this repo and nothing states it.\n85\t\n86\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n87\t\n88\t**Reviewer's note:** No mTLS, DPoP or proof-of-possession binding appears; support depends on the STS and services outside this repo\n89\t\n90\t(no evidence cited)\n91\t\n92\t## Your answer\n93\t\n94\tWrite this JSON to B03.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"B03\"):\n95\t```json\n96\t{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n97\t \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B03.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"pat